#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173275 :tutorial/content "For example, suppose we wish to disprove the following statement:\n\n$5n-1$ is odd for any $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$ \n\nOur statement can be written using formal notation as follows:\n![](images/d2/d22dcb4aa35870e9df2417316f7de327000f9b0cbd6758f79d4552d2a144cde1.svg)\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n![](images/32/3290200527bb59151b38e031c2b4a501784ccd6ca333bc7bef22ce5096ac6fce.svg)\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\n*To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that $5n-1$ is even.*\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=1$ suffices:\n\n*Notice that by substituting $n=1$ into the expression, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n-1 & =5(1)-1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*which is even.*\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\n*So, $n=1$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.*\n\nNow that we've figured out the details, let's write down the full argument." :tutorial/id #uuid "8199cce1-5561-58e7-98fa-8cdf64988b6b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8346 :tutorial/title "Disproving a Statement About Parity"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173297 :tutorial/content "To **disprove** a statement means to show that it is false. In this lesson, we will learn how to disprove universal statements.\n\nSuppose we wish to disprove a universal statement of the form \n\n$$\n\\forall x \\in U, \\: P(x),\n$$\n\nwhere $U$ is some universal set. To do that, we must demonstrate that the statement's negation is true. \n\nThe negation of this statement is\n\n$$\n\\neg \\big( \\forall x \\in U, \\: P(x) \\big) \\: \\equiv \\: \\exists x \\in U, \\: \\lnot P(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, to disprove a universal statement, finding one value of the variable that makes the negation true is sufficient. This value is called a **counterexample** to the original statement." :tutorial/id #uuid "ac5efd40-5d78-5a92-a14d-9b292d2cf5dc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9356 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173298 :tutorial/content "Disprove the following statement:\n\n*$5n-1$ is odd for any $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$*\n\nCounterexample:\n\n*To disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists an integer $n$ such that $5n-1$ is even.*\n\n*Notice that by substituting $n=1$ into the expression, we obtain*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5n-1 & =5(1)-1 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*which is even.*\n\n*So, $n=1$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.*" :tutorial/id #uuid "fe1dd960-2b39-501f-a632-d369a200c00c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9357 :tutorial/title "Stating the Full Counterexample"}]}
